-- C++ binding for anthill.prelude.String. Proposal 038 — host-specific spec
-- satisfaction lives separately from the language-agnostic spec in
-- stdlib/anthill/prelude/string.anthill.
--
-- WI-890: the C++ peer of `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/string.anthill`. Before this
-- file, `String` had a `TypeMapping` (its VALUES lower to `std::string`, in
-- cpp_std.anthill) but NO cpp binding block, so every one of its OPERATIONS was a loud
-- `unlowerable_operation` refusal (WI-886). This is the fill.
--
-- `host_fn` here is a C++ EXPRESSION TEMPLATE — `$1`, `$2`, … stand for the operation's
-- arguments (`docs/kernel-language.md` §10.2; `float.anthill` beside this file for the
-- fuller note). Each slot must appear EXACTLY ONCE, so an operation whose C++ reads an
-- operand twice binds it in a non-capturing lambda (`compare`) rather than a template
-- that would evaluate an already-lowered argument twice.
--
-- THE INDEX UNIT IS THE UNICODE SCALAR, not the byte (WI-884). `std::string` is BYTES
-- throughout, and the naive `s.find` / `s.substr` mapping answers in bytes — which
-- `substring(s, indexOf(s, sub), …)` then uses to cut the WRONG span on any string with
-- a multi-byte prefix, the exact defect WI-884 refused. `length`, `substring` and
-- `indexOf` are therefore realized by scalar-indexed helpers in
-- `rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/anthill_runtime.hpp` (`anthill::runtime::str_*`), which
-- is more than an expression template can carry; `replace`, `split`, `trim` and
-- `repeat` (which build a structured result) and `endsWith` (no `std::string::ends_with`
-- before C++20) join them. The `#include "anthill_runtime.hpp"` those spellings need
-- rides an `IncludeMapping` probe on `anthill::runtime::` in cpp_std.anthill, exactly as
-- the `std::` spellings' headers do; the header is copied beside every generated one.
--
-- NO `carrier` CLAUSE, load-bearing exactly as in `float.anthill`: `String`'s C++
-- spelling is `TypeMapping(anthill_type: "String", host_type: "std::string")` in
-- cpp_std.anthill, the PROFILE-OVERLAYABLE channel a `cpp20-stl` overlay can remap. A
-- `carrier { String: … }` clause is consulted ahead of that query and would silently
-- shadow the overlay.
--
-- toUpper / toLower ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and stay a loud codegen refusal. Full
-- Unicode case mapping (rust's `to_uppercase`/`to_lowercase` — locale-independent,
-- with multi-scalar expansions such as `ß` -> `SS`) has no C++17 standard-library
-- realization; `std::toupper` is ASCII-and-locale, and an ASCII-only mapping would be
-- exactly the silent non-ASCII divergence WI-884 refused. So the two operations refuse
-- at codegen naming themselves (`an_operation_with_no_body_and_no_cpp_realization…`),
-- which is the loudest available signal, until a profile that vendors an ICU-like
-- library adds them. This is the ticket's "the restriction is stated in the binding
-- block and the operations it cannot realize stay a loud refusal."
namespace anthill.prelude
  provides String language cpp
    operation_map {
      -- Ord (WI-876). `std::string`'s relational operators compare byte-by-byte as
      -- UNSIGNED char (`std::char_traits<char>::lt`), which for valid UTF-8 is code-point
      -- order — the same total order rust's `str: Ord` gives, so the two hosts agree.
      -- `compare` reads its operands twice, hence the lambda.
      compare: "[](const std::string& l, const std::string& r) { return static_cast<int64_t>((l > r) - (l < r)); }($1, $2)",
      gt: "($1 > $2)",
      gte: "($1 >= $2)",
      lt: "($1 < $2)",
      lte: "($1 <= $2)",
      max: "std::max($1, $2)",
      min: "std::min($1, $2)",

      -- Byte-safe as plain expressions: concatenation and the boolean queries answer the
      -- same for bytes and scalars because a match lands on a scalar boundary in valid
      -- UTF-8. `contains`/`startsWith` treat the empty pattern as present at position 0
      -- (`find("")` / `rfind("", 0)` both answer 0), matching the sort's laws.
      concat: "($1 + $2)",
      isEmpty: "$1.empty()",
      contains: "($1.find($2) != std::string::npos)",
      startsWith: "($1.rfind($2, 0) == 0)",

      -- Scalar-indexed or structure-building or double-reading — realized by the runtime
      -- helpers (see the header note above).
      length: "anthill::runtime::str_length($1)",
      indexOf: "anthill::runtime::str_index_of($1, $2)",
      substring: "anthill::runtime::str_substring($1, $2, $3)",
      endsWith: "anthill::runtime::str_ends_with($1, $2)",
      replace: "anthill::runtime::str_replace($1, $2, $3)",
      trim: "anthill::runtime::str_trim($1)",
      split: "anthill::runtime::str_split($1, $2)",
      repeat: "anthill::runtime::str_repeat($1, $2)"
    }
  end
end

-- NO SPEC-SATISFACTION FACTS (`fact Eq[T = String]`, `fact Ord[T = String]`, …),
-- like `float.anthill` beside this file and unlike the rust binding: a `provides` fact
-- enters coherence and dispatch, and the cpp-gen suite needs none of them (String's own
-- operations dispatch by carrier, not through a typeclass). This file asserts only what
-- it is for — the C++ spellings. The rust binding keeps its set because the rust
-- runtime's typer does dispatch `Ord`/`Eq` on `String`.
